  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cam Schlittler held Toronto to one run over six innings, and Spencer Jones supplied the power that made it stick.",
    "what_this_means": "They took the lead in the second and never gave it back. That sounds simple, but it's the kind of wire-to-wire control this team hasn't always managed. Entering the game three games back, they needed clean execution and they delivered it. Schlittler's 2.16 ERA now looks less like a rookie surprise and more like a real thing. The bullpen shut the door hard: Headrick struck out two, Blackburn was clean, Bednar got the save. Ten strikeouts against four hits is the kind of mismatch that builds confidence. They left runners on base in the first and fifth. Missed the bigger moment in that fifth frame. But Jones's two-run shot in the second was enough. This is what balanced looks like now: pitching that doesn't crack, enough offense to matter, relief arms that don't blink. The window to climb back into the division just stayed open because they didn't waste an inning on a night when everything lined up.",
    "what_to_watch": "Dylan Cease takes the ball tonight with a 2.42 ERA, and Ryan Weathers comes in off a 0.71 mark over his last four starts.",
